git format-patch already sends an empty commit as a patch with only
a message. The loss is on the applying side. am.sh runs git am with
--empty=drop, so it throws away every message without a diff. That is
how it skips cover letters and replies when you pipe in a whole
thread, but it also drops a plan commit.

The fix is "[PATCH] libexec/mail: Apply message-only commits as
empty" [1]. It keeps a message that has no diff when that message has
a Change-Id header. format-patch.sh puts that header on every commit's
patch, and never on a cover letter or a reply, so piping in a whole
thread still works. It also drops git's "-- " signature from those
messages, since with no diff nothing else would keep it out of the
commit. I tested it on a thread with a cover letter, an empty commit,
a commit with a diff, and a reply. Only the two commits were applied,
and the empty one kept its full message. A plain patch with a diff
and no Change-Id still applies as before.

I also need to correct my last message. just fetch only abandons
empty commits in ~/lab/mail.jj, and only ones you did not author. So
apply a plan in ~/lab/default.jj, not in the mail workspace.

The plan itself is posted as "[RFC PATCH 0/7] Write the commit rules
down once" [2], as seven empty commits on b/dev. With [1] applied,
this gets it:

    notmuch show --format=mbox --entire-thread=true \
        id:cover.1792269999.git.claude@p7x.dev |
        just am ~/lab/default.jj

Rewrite whatever you like with jj describe, then send it back by
format-patch.sh or as a reply. One caveat: git am cannot carry the
Change-Id into the commit, so your copies get new change ids, and I
will match them to mine by subject. The cover letter has one open
question, on whether the 72-column check should skip indented lines.

